        "role": "coach",
        "text": "Before coding, describe the algorithm you would try for the longest common substring of ABABC and BABCA. What do the pointers or states represent?",
        "action": "PROBE",
        "visual": null
      },
      {
        "role": "learner",
        "text": "I would use two pointers, one at the start of each string. If the characters match, I extend the current substring. Otherwise, I reset the count and move both pointers forward."
      },
      {
        "role": "coach",
        "text": "Can you clarify how you would handle the situation when the characters do not match? Specifically, what would you do with the pointers in that case?",
        "action": "CLARIFY",
        "visual": null
      },
      {
        "role": "learner",
        "text": "My method only examines positions where both pointers have advanced equally. It could miss a common substring starting at different offsets, such as BABC in the example."
      },
      {
        "role": "coach",
        "text": "How would you adjust your approach to ensure that you consider substrings starting at different offsets in both strings when the characters do not match?",
        "action": "CLARIFY",
        "visual": null
      },
      {
        "role": "learner",
        "text": "I could use a 2D table. dp[i][j] is the length of the common substring ending at the i-th character of s1 and j-th character of s2. When they match, use dp[i-1][j-1]+1; otherwise reset to zero. Track the maximum."
      },
      {
        "role": "coach",
        "text": "Your approach using a 2D table is solid, but how would you ensure that you correctly initialize the table and handle the boundaries when accessing dp[i-1][j-1]?",
        "action": "CHALLENGE",
        "visual": null
      },
      {
        "role": "learner",
        "text": "The reusable principle is to represent a problem using states defined by the endpoints of two sequences. Matching elements extend a contiguous run; mismatches break it. Considering every pair of endpoints avoids missing shifted alignments."
      },
      {
        "role": "coach",
        "text": "Your explanation of using states defined by endpoints is insightful, but how would you specifically initialize the 2D table and handle the boundaries to avoid accessing out-of-bounds indices when calculating dp[i][j]?",
        "action": "CHALLENGE",
        "visual": null
      },
      {
        "role": "learner",
        "text": "For two integer arrays, I can use the same dp[i][j] as the length of the common contiguous subarray ending at those indices. Equal integers extend the diagonal by one, unequal integers reset it to zero. I track the largest value."
      },
      {
        "role": "coach",
        "text": "Your understanding of using dp[i][j] to track the length of common contiguous subarrays is correct. How would you initialize the first row and first column of the dp table to handle the boundaries properly?",
